<section class="section log-section" id="case-studies" aria-labelledby="case-title"><p class="eyebrow">SELECTED CAREER NOTES / 03</p><h2 id="case-title">Work, scope, evidence.</h2><p class="section-lede">These case studies summarize responsibilities from the AI Enablement base resume. They do not identify employers or clients; the source resume does not publish them here. Measured outcomes are omitted where the source provides none.</p>
<article class="case-entry"><p class="entry-index">CASE NOTE 01 <span>AI ENABLEMENT / AGENTOPS</span></p><h3>Building practices for AI-assisted engineering</h3><dl><div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Engineering teams are adopting AI tools while practices, agent patterns, and operating expectations continue to change.</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Applied automation and AgentOps, including AI-assisted engineering patterns, agent design, LLM integration, observability, and fallback handling.</dd></div><div><dt>Work</dt><dd>Develop reusable approaches and coach teams as tools and practices evolve. Keep operational visibility and a fallback path part of the design.</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence boundary</dt><dd>The resume describes responsibilities, but does not state a verified before-and-after measure. No adoption, productivity, or quality result is claimed here.</dd></div></dl></article>
<article class="case-entry"><p class="entry-index">CASE NOTE 02 <span>QUALITY ENGINEERING</span></p><h3>Moving quality closer to delivery</h3><dl><div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Enterprise software delivery needs quality practices that connect development, test strategy, user acceptance, and release decisions.</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>QA and test strategy, shift-left practices, developer testing, automation, standards, UAT, defect management, and acquisition-related workstreams.</dd></div><div><dt>Work</dt><dd>Led quality engineering across these delivery activities, bringing testing and quality decisions into the software lifecycle.</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence boundary</dt><dd>The available resume summary does not provide a specific program, baseline, or measured result for public attribution. No defect reduction or release-speed claim is made.</dd></div></dl></article>
<article class="case-entry"><p class="entry-index">CASE NOTE 03 <span>PERFORMANCE ENGINEERING</span></p><h3>Understanding behavior under real conditions</h3><dl><div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Complex application environments need performance work that helps teams assess system behavior, risk, and readiness.</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Performance engineering across a broad application portfolio and enterprise environments.</dd></div><div><dt>Work</dt><dd>Helped teams reason about observed behavior, performance risk, and operational readiness across applications.</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence boundary</dt><dd>The source resume summary does not publish named systems, test conditions, or validated metrics. No capacity or latency improvement is asserted.</dd></div></dl></article></section>
